      "text": "To find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\), we first need to determine the endpoints of segment \\( s_2 \\) by translating the endpoints of segment \\( s_1 \\) by 2 units to the right and 3 units up. The original endpoints of segment \\( s_1 \\) are \\((4,1)\\) and \\((-8,5)\\).\n\nThe translation of a point \\((x, y)\\) by 2 units to the right and 3 units up results in the new point \\((x+2, y+3)\\). Applying this to the endpoints of segment \\( s_1 \\):\n\n1. For the point \\((4,1)\\):\n   \\[\n   (4 + 2, 1 + 3) = (6, 4)\n   \\]\n\n2. For the point \\((-8,5)\\):\n   \\[\n   (-8 + 2, 5 + 3) = (-6, 8)\n   \\]\n\nSo, the endpoints of segment \\( s_2 \\) are \\((6,4)\\) and \\((-6,8)\\).\n\nNext, we find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\) using the midpoint formula. The midpoint formula for two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\nSubstituting the coordinates of the endpoints of segment \\( s_2 \\):\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 + (-6)}{2}, \\frac{4 + 8}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{0}{2}, \\frac{12}{2} \\right) = (0, 6)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{(0,6)}\\).",
