      "text": "To find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\) obtained by translating segment \\( s_1 \\) by 2 units to the right and 3 units up, we first need to determine the coordinates of the endpoints of \\( s_2 \\).\n\nThe coordinates of the endpoints of segment \\( s_1 \\) are \\((4, 1)\\) and \\((-8, 5)\\). The translation involves adding 2 to the x-coordinate and 3 to the y-coordinate for each endpoint.\n\n1. Translate the endpoint \\((4, 1)\\):\n   \\[\n   (4 + 2, 1 + 3) = (6, 4)\n   \\]\n\n2. Translate the endpoint \\((-8, 5)\\):\n   \\[\n   (-8 + 2, 5 + 3) = (-6, 8)\n   \\]\n\nSo, the endpoints of segment \\( s_2 \\) are \\((6, 4)\\) and \\((-6, 8)\\).\n\nThe midpoint of a segment with endpoints \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by the formula:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nUsing the coordinates of the endpoints of \\( s_2 \\), we can find the midpoint:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 + (-6)}{2}, \\frac{4 + 8}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{0}{2}, \\frac{12}{2} \\right) = (0, 6)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{(0, 6)}\\).",
